window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__=(window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__||[]).concat([{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-011","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Allthe following are major recommended indications for IVIG EXCEPT 242 [Continuation on source page 253]","o":["replacement therapy for primary immunodeficiency disorders","Kawasaki disease","hepatitis A prophylaxis","i immune-mediated thrombocytopenia prophylaxis of infection following bone marrow transplantation"],"k":2,"ex":"For hepatitis A prophylaxis the recommended product is intramuscular immunoglobulin (IG), not IVIG. IVIG is reserved for replacement and immunomodulatory indications.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** IVIG is standard replacement therapy in primary immunodeficiency.\n\n- **B.** IVIG is the treatment of Kawasaki disease (with aspirin).\n- **D.** IVIG is used in immune thrombocytopenia and after bone-marrow transplantation; OCR merged two options here.\n\nNelson explanation:\n11.(C). in hepatitis A prophylaxis, intramuscular IG is recommended.\n\nHigh-yield:\nIVIG: Kawasaki, ITP, primary immunodeficiency, Guillain-Barré. IM IG: hepatitis A and measles post-exposure.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: options D and E appear merged in the original transcription; the key (C) is unaffected.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 252 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":11,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-013","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A toxoid is a modified bacterial toxin that is made non-toxic but still able to induce an active immune response against the toxin. Which of the following vaccine is a toxoid?","o":["hepatitis A","hepatitis B","pneumococcal","varicella","diphtheria"],"k":4,"ex":"Diphtheria vaccine is a toxoid: a toxin made harmless but still immunogenic. Tetanus is the other toxoid.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Hepatitis A is an inactivated virus.\n\n- **B.** Hepatitis B is a recombinant protein vaccine.\n- **C.** Pneumococcal vaccine is polysaccharide/conjugate.\n- **D.** Varicella is live attenuated.\n\nNelson explanation:\n13.(E). Toxoids are (tetanus and diphtheria).\n\nHigh-yield:\nToxoid vaccines: diphtheria and tetanus.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 253 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":13,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-014","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Hepatitis A vaccine, licensed for administration to children 12 mo of age and older. The 2 doses in the series should be separated by at least","o":["2 mo","4 mo","6 mo","1 yr","2 yr"],"k":2,"ex":"Hepatitis A vaccine is given from 12 months in 2 doses, with the second at least 6 months after the first (booster 6–18 months).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** 2 months is too short to give a booster response.\n\n- **B.** 4 months is too short.\n- **D.** 1 year is allowed but the minimum interval is 6 months.\n- **E.** 2 years is not required.\n\nNelson explanation:\n14.(C).\n\nHigh-yield:\nHepatitis A: ≥12 months, 2 doses at least 6 months apart.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 253 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":14,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-018","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which of the following vaccines is contraindicated for a patient with X-linked agammaglobulinemia?","o":["BCG","hepatitis B","DPT","MMR","varicella"],"k":0,"ex":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia is a humoral immunodeficiency, so live vaccines are avoided. BCG is a live bacterial vaccine and can cause disseminated disease.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Hepatitis B vaccine is inactivated recombinant and safe (response may be poor).\n\n- **C.** DPT is killed/toxoid and safe.\n- **D.** MMR is live: the curriculum rule would exclude it in humoral deficiency, although Nelson allows it in some antibody deficiencies.\n- **E.** Varicella is live: the same applies as for MMR.\n\nNelson explanation:\n18.(A). Oral polio, smallpox, live attenuated influenza, and BCG all these vaccines are\ncontraindicated for this patient.\n\nHigh-yield:\nNever give live vaccines in cellular or severe combined immunodeficiency; in antibody deficiency avoid live bacterial vaccines and OPV.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: the curriculum says live vaccines are contraindicated in humoral immunodeficiency, which would also cover MMR (D) and varicella (E). The Nelson key (A) is kept.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 254 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":18,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-019","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which of the following vaccines is contraindicated for a patient with chronic renal disease?","o":["pneumococcal","hepatitis B","live attenuated influenza","varicella","hepatitis A"],"k":2,"ex":"Live attenuated influenza vaccine (intranasal) is not used in chronic renal disease; the inactivated injectable vaccine is recommended instead.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Pneumococcal vaccine is indicated in chronic renal failure.\n\n- **B.** Hepatitis B vaccine is recommended in renal disease/dialysis.\n- **D.** Varicella is live but not contraindicated unless the child is immunosuppressed.\n- **E.** Hepatitis A is inactivated and safe.\n\nNelson explanation:\n19.(C).\n\nHigh-yield:\nChronic renal failure: give inactivated influenza, pneumococcal and hepatitis B vaccines.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 254 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":19,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-028","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"Bulbar conjunctivitis in a child with FUO suggests","o":["leptospirosis","coxsackievirus infection","tuberculosis","infectious mononucleosis","lymphogranuloma venereum"],"k":0,"ex":"Bulbar (non-exudative) conjunctival injection in a child with prolonged fever points to leptospirosis (or Kawasaki disease). Palpebral conjunctivitis is the pattern seen in the other options.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Coxsackievirus causes palpebral conjunctivitis.\n\n- **C.** Tuberculosis is associated with palpebral conjunctivitis, not bulbar.\n- **D.** Infectious mononucleosis gives palpebral/periorbital oedema.\n- **E.** Lymphogranuloma venereum is a cause of palpebral (Parinaud-type) conjunctivitis.\n\nNelson explanation:\n28.(A). Palpebral conjunctivitis in a febrile patient may be a clue to measles,\ncoxsackievirus\ninfection,\ntuberculosis,\ninfectious\nmononucleosis,\nlymphogranulomavenereum, or cat-scratch disease. In contrast, bulbar conjunctivitis in\na child with FUO suggests Kawasaki disease or leptospirosis.\n29.(0). Multiple or repeated blood cultures may be required to detect bacteremia\nassociated with infective endocarditis, osteomyelitis, or deep-seated abscesses.\n\nHigh-yield:\nBulbar conjunctivitis + fever = leptospirosis or Kawasaki disease.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 256 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":28,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-033","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Relative bradycardia (when the pulse rate remains low in the presence of fever) can accompany all the following conditions ExCEPT","o":["typhoid fever","brucellosis","leptospirosis","visceral leishmaniasis","drug fever"],"k":3,"ex":"Relative bradycardia (pulse rate lower than expected for the fever) occurs in typhoid, brucellosis, leptospirosis and drug fever. It is not a feature of visceral leishmaniasis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Typhoid fever classically shows relative bradycardia (curriculum).\n\n- **B.** Brucellosis can show it.\n- **C.** Leptospirosis can show it.\n- **E.** Drug fever can show it.\n\nNelson explanation:\n33.(D).\n\nHigh-yield:\nRelative bradycardia: typhoid, brucella, leptospirosis, drug fever.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 257 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":33,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-045","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Which of the following represent a major criterion for the diagnosis of staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome?","o":["rash","conjunctival hyperemia","myalgia","thrombocytopenia","vomiting"],"k":0,"ex":"Staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome has three major criteria, all required: fever, hypotension and rash (erythroderma with later desquamation). Rash is the only major criterion listed.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Mucous-membrane hyperemia is a minor criterion.\n\n- **C.** Myalgia is a minor criterion (muscle involvement).\n- **D.** Thrombocytopenia is a minor criterion.\n- **E.** Vomiting/diarrhoea is a minor criterion.\n\nNelson explanation:\n45.(A).\nMAJOR CRITERIA (ALL REQUIRED)\n1. Acute fever; temperature >38.8°C (101.8°F)\nHypotension\n3.\nRash (erythroderma with convalescent desquamation)\nMINOR CRITERIA (ANY 3 OR MORE)\n1.\nMucous membrane inflammation (vaginal, oropharyngeal or conjunctival\nhyperemia, strawberry tongue)\n2.\nVomiting, diarrhea\n3.\nLiver abnormalities (bilirubin or transaminase greater than twice upper limit of\nnormal)\n4.\nRenal abnormalities (urea nitrogen or creatinine greater than twice upper limit\nof normal, or greater than 5 white blood cells per high-power field)\n5.\nMuscle abnormalities (myalgia or creatinine phosphokinase greater than twice\nupper limit of normal)\n6.\nCentral nervous system abnormalities (alteration in consciousness without\nfocal neurologic signs)\n7. Thrombocytopenia (100,000/mm3 or less)\n\nHigh-yield:\nTSS: major = fever + hypotension + rash; minor = any 3 organ systems (≥3).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 259 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":45,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-049","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Scarlet fever is an upper respiratory tract infection associated with a characteristic rash. Of the following, which statement is TRUE?","o":["it is caused by an infection with pyrogenic endotoxin producing group A streptococcus","the rash appears 96 hr after onset of symptoms","the rash begins to fade after 1-2 weeks","before desquamation, the reddened papillae are prominent, giving the tongue a strawberry appearance","the milder form can be confused with Kawasaki disease"],"k":4,"ex":"A mild scarlet fever can be mistaken for viral exanthems, Kawasaki disease or drug eruptions, because the rash and tongue changes overlap.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** The toxin is an exotoxin (erythrogenic/pyrogenic exotoxin), not an endotoxin.\n\n- **B.** The rash appears within 12–48 hours of onset, not at 96 hours.\n- **C.** The rash fades after 3–4 days and is followed by desquamation, not after 1–2 weeks.\n- **D.** The tongue goes from white-coated to red strawberry (white strawberry on day 1, red on day 3); the option's timing wording is not accepted by the source.\n\nNelson explanation:\n49.(E). It is caused by an infection with pyrogenic exotoxin (erythrogenic toxin)-\nproducing GAS. The rash appears within 24-48 hr after onset of symptoms, although it\nmay appear with the first signs of illness. After 3-4 days, the rash begins to fade and is\nfollowed by desquamation. After desquamation, the reddened papillae are prominent,\ngiving the tongue a strawberry appearance. The milder form can be confused with viral\nexanthems, Kawasaki disease, and drug eruptions.\n50.(c). Nonbullous impetigo is the more common form. The lesions may occur\nanywhere but are most common on the face and extremities. Regional lymphadenitis is\ncommon. Nonbullous impetigo is generally not accompanied by fever or other systemic\nsigns or symptoms. Bullous impetigo is less common and occurs most often in neonates\nand young infants. The usual distribution involves the face, buttocks, trunk, and\nperineum.\n\nHigh-yield:\nScarlet fever: exotoxin, rash within 12–48 h, strawberry tongue, treat with penicillin V for 10 days.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 260 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":49,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-052","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"Because no clinical or laboratory finding is pathognomonic for acute rheumatic fever, 5 major and 4 minor criteria with evidence of recent group A streptococcus infection are required for the diagnosis. Migratory polyarthritis represent one of the major criteria, which of the following statements is TRUE regarding this major criterion?","o":["occurs in approximately 50% of patients 250 [Continuation on source page 261] typically involves small joints findings","rheumatic arthritis is almost deforming","there is often a proportional relationship between the severity of arthritis and the severity of cardiac involvement"],"k":2,"ex":"In rheumatic arthritis the joints involved are the large joints (knees, ankles, wrists, elbows), it is migratory, affects about 75% of patients and is non-deforming. Severity runs inversely to carditis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** About 75%, not 50%, of patients have arthritis.\n\n- **D.** Rheumatic arthritis almost never deforms the joint.\n- **E.** The relationship is inverse, not proportional (milder carditis when arthritis is severe).\n\nNelson explanation:\n52.(C). Arthritis occurs in approximately 75% of patients with acute rheumatic fever\nand typically involves larger joints, particularly the knees, ankles, wrists, and elbows.\nInvolvement of the spine, small joints of the hands and feet, or hips is uncommon.\nRheumatic arthritis is almost never deforming. There is often an inverse relationship\nbetween the severity of arthritis and the severity of cardiac involvement.\n\nHigh-yield:\nRheumatic arthritis: large joints, migratory, non-deforming, dramatic response to salicylates.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: the OCR damaged options B and C (only \"typically involves small joints / findings\" survives), so the key (C) cannot be mapped to its text. Nelson's explanation states the true statement is \"large joints\". Key preserved.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 260 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":52,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-058","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Group B streptococcus (GBS), or Streptococcus agalactiae, is a major cause of neonatal bacterial sepsis. Of the following, the MosT common syndrome associated with childhood GBS disease beyond early infancy is","o":["bacteremia without a focus","meningitis",". ventriculitis","septic arthritis","pneumonia"],"k":0,"ex":"Beyond early infancy invasive group B streptococcal disease is uncommon, and its most common presentation is bacteremia without a focus.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Meningitis (OCR merged with \"ventriculitis\") is mainly a neonatal late-onset presentation.\n\n- **D.** Septic arthritis is uncommon.\n- **E.** Pneumonia is mainly early-onset neonatal disease.\n\nNelson explanation:\n58.(A). Early-onset neonatal GBs disease presents as sepsis; pneumonia and meningitis.\nLate-onset neonatal GBs disease most commonly manifests as bacteremia (45-65%)\nand meningitis (25-35%). Invasive GBS disease in children beyond early infancy is\nuncommon. Bacteremia without a focus is the most common syndrome associated\nwith childhood GBs disease beyond early infancy.\n\nHigh-yield:\nNeonatal GBS: early onset = sepsis/pneumonia; late onset = bacteremia/meningitis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: options B and C appear merged (\"meningitis . ventriculitis\"); the key (A) is unaffected.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 262 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":58,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-059","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Recommended duration of therapy for manifestations of group B streptococcus are as follow","o":["bacteremia without a focus: 7 days","meningitis: 3-6 weeks","ventriculitis: at least 8 weeks","septic arthritis: 3-4 weeks","osteomyelitis: 6-8 weeks"],"k":3,"ex":"Septic arthritis due to group B streptococcus is treated for 3–4 weeks.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Bacteremia without a focus needs about 10 days, not 7.\n\n- **B.** Meningitis needs 2–3 weeks.\n- **C.** Ventriculitis needs at least 4 weeks, not 8.\n- **E.** Osteomyelitis needs 3–4 weeks, not 6–8.\n\nNelson explanation:\n59.(D).\n0\nbacteremia without a focus 10 days\n0\nmeningitis 2-3 wk\n0\nventriculitis at least 4 wk\n0\nseptic arthritis 3-4 wkase\n0\nosteomyelitis 3-4 wkas\n\nHigh-yield:\nGBS: bacteremia 10 d, meningitis 2–3 wk, ventriculitis ≥4 wk, arthritis/osteomyelitis 3–4 wk.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 262 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":59,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-061","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A painless, slow-growing, hard mass producing cutaneous fistulas, a condition commonly known as lumpy jaw is usually caused by","o":["Staphylococcus","Actinomyces","Nocardia","Yersinia","Leptospira"],"k":1,"ex":"\"Lumpy jaw\" is cervicofacial actinomycosis: a painless, slow-growing hard mass with cutaneous fistulas draining sulfur granules, usually after dental disease or trauma. The cause is Actinomyces.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Staphylococcus causes acute pyogenic infections, not sulfur-granule fistulas.\n\n- **C.** Nocardia causes pulmonary/skin disease in immunocompromised patients.\n- **D.** Yersinia causes enterocolitis and mesenteric adenitis.\n- **E.** Leptospira causes leptospirosis.\n\nNelson explanation:\n61.(B). Three major forms of actinomycosis-cervicofacial, abdominal and pelvic, and\npulmonary. In the patient with cervicofacial actinomycosis, there is often a history of\noral trauma, oral surgery, dental procedures, or caries, facilitating entry of organisms\ninto cervicofacial tissues. Cervicofacial actinomycosis usually manifests as a painless,\nslow-growing, hard mass and can produce cutaneous fistulas, a condition commonly\nknown as lumpy jaw. Less frequently, cervicofacial actinomycosis manifests clinically as\nan acute pyogenic infection with a tender, fluctuant mass with trismus, firm swelling.\nand fistulas with drainage containing the characteristic sulfur granules.\n\nHigh-yield:\nActinomyces: anaerobic gram-positive branching rods; sulfur granules.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 262 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":61,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-063","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which of the following is a gram negative bacterium?","o":["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Actinomyces","Haemophilus influenza","Nocardia"],"k":3,"ex":"Haemophilus influenzae is a gram-negative coccobacillus.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Staphylococcus aureus is gram-positive cocci.\n\n- **B.** Streptococcus pneumoniae is a gram-positive diplococcus.\n- **C.** Actinomyces is a gram-positive branching anaerobe.\n- **E.** Nocardia is a gram-positive, weakly acid-fast branching rod.\n\nNelson explanation:\n63.(D). Actinomyces, staphylococcus, streptococcus, diphtheria, enterococcus, listeria\nand nocardia are gram positive.\n\nHigh-yield:\nGram-negative: Haemophilus, Neisseria, Salmonella, E. coli, Bordetella (curriculum).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 263 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":63,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-079","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Hospital discharge of infants with pertussis is appropriate in all the following circumstances EXCEPT","o":["over a 24-hr period disease severity is unchanged or diminished","intervention is not required during paroxysms","nutrition is adequate","no complication has occurred","parents are adequately prepared for care at home"],"k":0,"ex":"Discharge of an infant with pertussis requires the disease to be stable or improving over 48 hours, not 24 hours. The statement in A is therefore the exception.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** No intervention (suction, oxygen) should be needed during paroxysms.\n\n- **C.** Adequate nutrition is required before discharge.\n- **D.** No complication such as pneumonia or apnea should have occurred.\n- **E.** Parents must be prepared to care for the child at home.\n\nNelson explanation:\n79.(A). Over a 48-hr period disease severity is unchanged or diminished.\n\nHigh-yield:\nAdmit young infants with pertussis (apnea, cyanosis, poor feeding); discharge when stable for 48 h.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 266 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":79,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-080","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The preferable antibiotic in neonate with pertussis is","o":["azithromycin","erythromycin","clarithromycin","trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","amoxycilline"],"k":0,"ex":"Azithromycin (10 mg/kg/day for 5 days) is the preferred macrolide in neonates with pertussis because erythromycin and clarithromycin carry a risk of infantile pyloric stenosis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Erythromycin is not preferred in neonates (pyloric stenosis).\n\n- **C.** Clarithromycin is not recommended in neonates.\n- **D.** TMP-SMX is contraindicated under 2 months (kernicterus risk).\n- **E.** Amoxicillin is not effective against Bordetella pertussis.\n\nNelson explanation:\n80.(A).\n0\nAzithromycin: Recommended agent 10 mg/kg/day in a single dose for 5 days\n0\nErythromycin: Not preferred\n0\nClarithromycin: Not recommended\no trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole: Contraindicated for infants <2 mo of age\n\nHigh-yield:\nPertussis: macrolide (azithromycin in young infants); treat contacts.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 266 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":80,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-081","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Echocardiography should be performed in critically ill infants with pertussis to detect presence of","o":["myocarditis","heart failure","congenital heart defect","pulmonary hypertension","right atrial dilatation"],"k":3,"ex":"Pulmonary hypertension and secondary bacterial pneumonia cause most pertussis deaths in young infants, so echocardiography is done in critically ill infants to detect pulmonary hypertension.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Myocarditis is rare in pertussis (a rare complication per curriculum).\n\n- **B.** Heart failure is not the targeted finding.\n- **C.** Congenital heart defect is not part of pertussis.\n- **E.** Right atrial dilatation is not the targeted finding.\n\nNelson explanation:\n81.(D). Progressive pulmonary hypertension in very young infants and secondary\nbacterial pneumonia are severe complications of pertussis and are the usual causes of\ndeath. Echocardiography should be performed in critically ill infants with pertussis to\ndetect presence of pulmonary hypertension and to intervene expeditiously.\n\nHigh-yield:\nSevere infant pertussis: apnea, pulmonary hypertension, pneumonia, marked leukocytosis (lymphocytosis).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 266 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":81,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-082","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The least common clinical feature of typhoid fever in children is","o":["diarrhea","abdominal pain","pallor","splenomegaly","headache"],"k":4,"ex":"Headache is the least frequent of the listed features in children with typhoid (about 4%). Diarrhoea (36%), abdominal pain (21%), pallor (20%) and splenomegaly (17%) are more frequent.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Diarrhoea is the commonest of the listed (36%).\n\n- **B.** Abdominal pain is more frequent (21%).\n- **C.** Pallor is more frequent (20%).\n- **D.** Splenomegaly is more frequent (17%).\n\nNelson explanation:\n82.(E). Diarrhea 36%, abdominal pain 21%, pallor 20%, splenomegaly 17% and\nheadache 4%.\n\nHigh-yield:\nTyphoid: fever with relative bradycardia, abdominal pain, constipation/diarrhoea, rose spots, splenomegaly.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Curriculum lists headache as a symptom of typhoid but does not give frequencies; the key rests on Nelson percentages. Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 266 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":82,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-084","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Regarding diagnosis of typhoid fever, one of the following is TRUE","o":["blood cultures are positive in 65-80% of the patients","urine culture results become positive within the 1st wk","thrombocytosis may be a marker of severe illness","diagnosis by Widal test alone is prone to error","leukocytosis is rare in young children"],"k":3,"ex":"The Widal test alone is unreliable (false positives and negatives), so diagnosis rests on cultures.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Blood cultures are positive in about 40–60% (not 65–80%) when taken early.\n\n- **B.** Stool and urine cultures turn positive only after the first week.\n- **C.** Thrombocytopenia (not thrombocytosis) may mark severe disease with DIC.\n- **E.** Leukocytosis is common in young children; leukopenia is typical in older ones.\n\nNelson explanation:\n84.(D). Results of blood cultures are positive in 40-60% of the patients seen early in the\ncourse of the disease, and stool and urine culture results become positive after the 1st\nwk. Thrombocytopenia may be a marker of severe illness and may accompany\ndisseminated intravascular coagulopathy. Blood leukocyte counts are frequently low in\nrelation to the fever and toxicity, there is a wide range in counts; in younger children\nleukocytosis is common and may reach 20,000-25,000 cells/μl.\n\nHigh-yield:\nTyphoid: blood culture in week 1, stool culture in week 2; bone marrow culture is most sensitive.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 267 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":84,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-091","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The genus Yersinia is a member of the family Enterobacteriaceae and comprises more than 14 named species, 3 of which are established as human pathogens. Which of the following is MOsT often associated with mesenteric lymphadenitis?","o":["Yersinia enterocolitica","Yersinia pseudotuberculosis","Yersinia pestis","Yersinia mollaretii","Yersinia rohdei"],"k":1,"ex":"Yersinia pseudotuberculosis is the species most often linked to mesenteric lymphadenitis. Y. enterocolitica is the commonest species overall but causes enterocolitis with pseudo-appendicitis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Y. enterocolitica causes fever, abdominal pain mimicking appendicitis and diarrhoea.\n\n- **C.** Y. pestis causes plague (bubonic: acute febrile lymphadenitis).\n- **D.** Y. mollaretii is rarely pathogenic.\n- **E.** Y. rohdei is rarely pathogenic.\n\nNelson explanation:\n91.(B). Yersinia enterocolitica is by far the most common Yersinia species causing\nhuman disease and it produces fever, abdominal pain that can mimic appendicitis, and\ndiarrhea, Yersinia pseudotuberculosis is most often associated with mesenteric\nlymphadenitis. Yersinia pestis is the agent of plague and most commonly causes an\nacute febrile lymphadenitis (bubonic plague) and less commonly occurs as septicemic,\npneumonic, pharyngeal, or meningeal plague. Other Yersinia organisms are uncommon\ncauses of infections of humans.\n277\n[Source page 288]\n\nHigh-yield:\nY. pseudotuberculosis = mesenteric adenitis; Y. enterocolitica = enterocolitis; Y. pestis = plague.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 268 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":91,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-094","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Tularemia is a zoonotic infection caused by the gram-negative bacterium Francisella tularensis. Of the following, the MOsT common forms of tularemia diagnosed in children is","o":["ulceroglandular","pneumonia","oropharyngeal","oculoglandular","typhoidal"],"k":0,"ex":"Ulceroglandular and glandular forms are the commonest presentations of tularemia in children, typically after a tick bite with a painful ulcer and cervical or auricular nodes.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Pneumonic tularemia follows inhalation and is less common in children.\n\n- **C.** Oropharyngeal tularemia follows ingestion and is uncommon.\n- **D.** Oculoglandular disease is rare.\n- **E.** Typhoidal tularemia is rare.\n\nNelson explanation:\n94.(A). Ulceroglandular and glandular disease are the 2 most common forms of\ntularemia diagnosed in children. The most common glands involved are the cervical or\nposterior auricular nodes owing to a tick bite on the head or neck. If an ulcer is present,\nit is erythematous and painful and may last from 1-3 wk. The ulcer is located at the\nportal of entry. After the ulcer develops, regional lymphadenopathy ensues.\n\nHigh-yield:\nTularemia: Francisella tularensis, tick/rabbit exposure, ulceroglandular form commonest.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 269 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":94,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-104","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"In neonates with HSV infection including CNS involvement, to improve for","o":["3 mo","6 mo","9mo","12 mo","18 mo"],"k":1,"ex":"After intravenous acyclovir for neonatal HSV with CNS involvement, suppressive oral acyclovir for 6 months improves neurodevelopmental outcome.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** 3 months is shorter than the proven benefit.\n\n- **C.** 9 months is not the studied duration.\n- **D.** 12 months is not the studied duration.\n- **E.** 18 months is not the studied duration.\n\nNelson explanation:\n104.(B). The use of suppressive therapy with oral acyclovir for 6 mo has been\ndemonstrated to improve neurodevelopmental outcome.\n\nHigh-yield:\nNeonatal HSV: IV acyclovir for 14–21 days, then oral suppression for 6 months.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: the stem is incomplete (\"to improve for\"); the key (B) comes from Nelson's explanation.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 271 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":104,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-105","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Acyclovir is a safe and effective therapy for herpes simplex virus (Hsv) infections. All the following are true EXCEPT","o":["activity against CMV is less pronounced","activity against Epstein-barr virus is modest, both in vitro and clinically","acyclovir therapy in a nursing mother is not a contraindication to breastfeeding","main route of elimination is hepatic","high doses of acyclovir are associated with neurotoxicity"],"k":3,"ex":"Acyclovir is eliminated mainly by the kidneys, so doses need adjusting in renal impairment. The statement that elimination is hepatic is false.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Activity against CMV is weak.\n\n- **B.** Activity against EBV is modest.\n- **C.** Acyclovir is compatible with breastfeeding.\n- **E.** High doses can cause neurotoxicity (and crystal nephropathy if infused fast).\n\nNelson explanation:\n105.(D). The main route of elimination is renal, and dosage adjustments are necessary\nfor renal insufficiency. Toxicity is observed typically only in exceptional circumstances:\nfor example, if administered by rapid infusion to a dehydrated patient or a patient with\nunderlying renal insufficiency, acyclovir can crystallize in renal tubules and produce a\nreversible obstructive uropathy.\n278\n[Source page 289]\n\nHigh-yield:\nAcyclovir: renal excretion, hydrate well, avoid rapid infusion.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 271 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":105,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-106","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Ribavirin is a guanosine analog that has broad-spectrum activity against a variety of viruses, particularly RNA viruses. All the following are true ExCEPT","o":["its precise mechanism of action is incompletely understood","aerosolized ribavirin effective for parainfluenza, influenza, and measles infections","ribavirin is generally nontoxic, particularly when administered by aerosol","ribavirin and its metabolites concentrate in hepatocytes","conjunctivitis and bronchospasm have been reported following exposure to aerosolized drug"],"k":3,"ex":"Ribavirin and its metabolites concentrate in red blood cells, persist for weeks and may cause haemolytic anaemia, not hepatocyte accumulation.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** The mechanism is incompletely understood.\n\n- **B.** Aerosolised ribavirin is active against parainfluenza, influenza and measles.\n- **C.** It is generally non-toxic, especially by aerosol.\n- **E.** Conjunctivitis and bronchospasm can follow aerosol exposure.\n\nNelson explanation:\n106.(D). Ribavirin and its metabolites concentrate in red blood cells and can persist for\nseveral weeks and, in rare instances, may be associated with anemia.\n\nHigh-yield:\nRibavirin: RSV and some RNA viruses; side effect = haemolytic anaemia; teratogenic.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 271 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":106,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-107","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The portal of entry of measles virus is through the respiratory tract or conjunctivae following contact with large droplets or small-droplet aerosols in which the virus is suspended. Patients with measles are infectious C3 days before to up to 4-6 days after the onset of rash + 261 [Continuation on source page 272] coryza, conjunctivitis, and a prominent exanthem. All the following are true EXCEPT","o":["incubation period is 8-12 days","Koplik spots have been reported in 50-70% of measles cases","t the rash begins on the forehead, behind the ears, and on the upper neck","the rash fades over about 3 days in the same progression as it evolved in more severe cases, generalized lymphadenopathy may be present"],"k":2,"ex":"The question asks for the one false statement about measles. The curriculum describes the rash as starting behind the ears, on the face and head, then spreading downward in 24 hours, so option C is not a false statement.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Nelson gives 8–12 days; the curriculum gives 10–14 days.\n\n- **B.** Koplik spots are pathognomonic; the 50–70% figure is Nelson-specific.\n- **D.** The rash fades in the same order as it appeared (OCR merged D with the lymphadenopathy sentence).\n\nNelson explanation:\n107.(C).\n\nHigh-yield:\nMeasles: cough, coryza, conjunctivitis; Koplik spots; rash starts behind the ears/face and spreads downward.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: the key (C) conflicts with the curriculum, which states the rash starts behind the ears, face and head. Option A (8–12 days) also differs from the curriculum (10–14 days). Option D is merged with another sentence in the OCR. Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 271 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":107,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-110","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) is a chronic complication of measles with a delayed onset and an outcome that is nearly always fatal. SSPE is characterized by","o":["females are affected twice as often as males","clinical manifestations begin insidiously 3-6 yr after primary measles infection","the hallmark of the 1” stage is massive myoclonus","the 3'd stage is characterized by loss of critical centers","clinical trials using isoprinosine suggest significant benefit"],"k":4,"ex":"The key states that isoprinosine trials suggest a significant benefit in SSPE. The other options contain wrong facts: SSPE affects males more, begins 7–13 years after measles, and massive myoclonus is the hallmark of stage 2.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Males are affected twice as often as females.\n\n- **B.** Onset is 7–13 years after measles, not 3–6.\n- **C.** Massive myoclonus characterises stage 2, not stage 1.\n- **D.** Loss of vital centres (breathing, heart rate, BP) is stage 4, not stage 3.\n\nNelson explanation:\n110.(E). Males are affected twice as often as females, and there appear to be more\ncases reported from rural than urban populations. Clinical manifestations of SspE begin\ninsidiously 7-13 yr after primary measles infection. The hallmark of the 2nd stage is\nmassive myoclonus, which coincides with extension of the inflammatory process site to\ndeeper structures in the brain, including the basal ganglia. The 4th stage is\ncharacterized by loss of critical centers that support breathing, heart rate, and blood\npressure. Death soon ensues.\n\nHigh-yield:\nSSPE: rare late complication of measles (about 1:100,000), progressive, usually fatal.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: the curriculum treats SSPE as supportive only and does not mention isoprinosine; Nelson's explanation does not address option E. Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 272 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":110,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-111","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The MosT common finding among infants with congenital rubella syndrome is","o":["psychomotor retardation","cataracts","deafness","patent ductus arteriosus","neonatal purpura"],"k":2,"ex":"Sensorineural deafness is the single most common finding in congenital rubella syndrome.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Developmental delay occurs but less often than deafness.\n\n- **B.** Cataract is classic but less common than deafness.\n- **D.** PDA (with pulmonary stenosis) is the commonest heart lesion, not the commonest finding overall.\n- **E.** Neonatal purpura/blueberry-muffin rash is transient.\n\nNelson explanation:\n111.(C). Nerve deafness is the single most common finding among infants with\ncongenital rubella syndrome.\n\nHigh-yield:\nCongenital rubella: deafness, cataract, heart disease (PDA, PS), blueberry-muffin rash, IUGR.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 272 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":111,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-112","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Mumps virus is in the family Paramyxoviridae and the genus Rubulavirus. Mumps is characterized by","o":["incubation period ranges from 7-11 days","unilateral parotitis rarely becomes bilateral","pale opening of the Stensen duct","parotid swelling peaks in approximately 7 days","a morbilliform rash is rarely seen"],"k":4,"ex":"Mumps is characterised by parotitis that is often unilateral first and bilateral later, with a morbilliform rash rarely seen.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Incubation is about 12–25 days (curriculum 14–21 days), not 7–11.\n\n- **B.** Unilateral parotitis becomes bilateral in about 70%.\n- **C.** The Stensen duct opening is red and swollen, not pale.\n- **D.** Parotid swelling peaks at about 3 days (OCR merged D with option E).\n\nNelson explanation:\n112.(E). The incubation period for mumps ranges from 12-25 days but is usually 16-18\ndays. Parotitis may be unilateral initially but becomes bilateral in approximately 70% of\ncases. The opening of the Stensen duct may be red and edematous. The parotid\nswelling peaks in approximately 3 days and then gradually subsides over 7 days.\n\nHigh-yield:\nMumps: parotitis (uni- then bilateral), incubation 14–21 days, complications orchitis and meningitis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 272 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":112,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-113","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The MOsT common complication of mumps is","o":["meningitis 262 [Continuation on source page 273]","conjunctivitis","optic neuritis","pneumonia","thrombocytopenia"],"k":0,"ex":"Meningitis (with or without encephalitis) and gonadal involvement are the commonest mumps complications.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Conjunctivitis is uncommon.\n\n- **C.** Optic neuritis is uncommon.\n- **D.** Pneumonia is uncommon.\n- **E.** Thrombocytopenia is uncommon.\n\nNelson explanation:\n113.(A). The most common complications of mumps are meningitis, with or without\nencephalitis, and\ngonadal involvement. Uncommon complications include\nconjunctivitis,optic\nneuritis,\npneumonia, nephritis, pancreatitis, and\nthrombocytopenia.\n\nHigh-yield:\nMumps complications: meningitis, orchitis (post-pubertal), oophoritis, pancreatitis, SNHL.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 272 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":113,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-114","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Intrauterine mumps infection have been associated with","o":["glaucoma","limb anomalies","pulmonary stenosis","renal agenesis","no fetal malformations"],"k":4,"ex":"Intrauterine mumps infection has not been linked to fetal malformations, unlike rubella.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Glaucoma is a rubella feature.\n\n- **B.** Limb anomalies are not linked to mumps (varicella can cause them).\n- **C.** Pulmonary stenosis is a rubella feature.\n- **D.** Renal agenesis is not linked to mumps.\n\nNelson explanation:\n114.(E). No fetal malformations have been associated with intrauterine mumps\ninfection.\n\nHigh-yield:\nCongenital malformation viruses: rubella, CMV, varicella, Zika; not mumps.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 273 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":114,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-115","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Mumps virus is neurotropic and is thought to enter the CNs via the choroid plexus and infect the choroidal epithelium and ependymal cells. CNs involvement is characterized by","o":["symptomatic CNS involvement occurs in 40-60% of infected individuals","encephalitis most commonly manifests 10 days after the parotitis","CNS symptoms usually resolve in 3 days","CSF protein content is usually elevated","facial palsy is a less-common CNS complication"],"k":4,"ex":"Facial palsy is a less common CNS complication of mumps. The other options give wrong numbers: symptomatic CNS involvement affects 10–30%, meningoencephalitis appears about 5 days after parotitis and resolves in 7–10 days.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Symptomatic involvement is 10–30% (CSF pleocytosis alone is 40–60%).\n\n- **B.** It most commonly appears about 5 days after parotitis, not 10.\n- **C.** Symptoms typically resolve in 7–10 days, not 3.\n- **D.** CSF protein is normal or only mildly elevated.\n\nNelson explanation:\n115.(E). Symptomatic CNS involvement occurs in 10-30% of infected individuals, but\nCSF pleocytosis has been found in 40-60% of patients with mumps parotitis. The\nmeningoencephalitis may occur before, along with, or following the parotitis. It most\ncommonly manifests 5 days after the parotitis. In typical cases, symptoms resolve in 7-\n10 days. The CSF protein content is normal or mildly elevated. Less-common CNS\ncomplications of mumps include transverse myelitis, aqueductal stenosis, and facial\npalsy.\n\nHigh-yield:\nMumps meningitis: benign, CSF lymphocytic pleocytosis, normal/mild protein, glucose normal or low.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 273 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":115,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-116","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Hematogenous dissemination of HSV to the central nervous system occur in","o":["neonates","individuals with eczema","severely malnourished children","infants on steroids","all the above"],"k":0,"ex":"Haematogenous spread of HSV to the central nervous system occurs only in neonates (neonatal HSV encephalitis). In older individuals CNS spread is neural/retrograde.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** In eczema herpeticum the virus disseminates in skin without haematogenous CNS spread.\n\n- **C.** Malnourished children develop mucocutaneous disease, not haematogenous CNS spread.\n- **D.** Steroid use increases severity but not haematogenous CNS spread.\n- **E.** Only A is correct.\n\nNelson explanation:\n116.(A). Hematogenous dissemination of virus to the central nervous system appears\nto only occur in neonates.\n\nHigh-yield:\nNeonatal HSV: skin-eye-mouth, CNS, or disseminated; acyclovir.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 273 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":116,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-120","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"In patients with neonatal HSV infection who receive IV treatment for 2-3 weeks then suppressive treatment for 6 months, they should be monitored by","o":["absolute neutrophil count ANC","liver function tests","renal function tests","platelet count","Hb%"],"k":0,"ex":"Prolonged oral acyclovir suppression can cause neutropenia, so the absolute neutrophil count is monitored (at weeks 2 and 4, then monthly).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Liver function is not the main toxicity monitored.\n\n- **C.** Renal function matters with IV acyclovir (crystal nephropathy) but is not the monitoring needed during oral suppression.\n- **D.** Platelets are not the main toxicity.\n- **E.** Haemoglobin is not the main toxicity.\n\nNelson explanation:\n120.(A). The absolute neutrophil count should be measured at weeks 2 and 4 after\ninitiation treatment and then monthly.\n\nHigh-yield:\nAcyclovir suppression: monitor ANC; IV acyclovir: hydrate and watch renal function.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 274 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":120,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-121","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"Varicella-zoster virus (VzV) causes primary, latent, and recurrent infections. All the following statements are true ExCEPT","o":["varicella is a serious disease in young infants","within households, transmission of VZV occurs at a rate of 65-86%","Herpes zoster is more common in winter","the lifetime risk for herpes zoster for individuals with a history of varicella is 20- 30%","Herpes zoster is very rare in healthy children younger than 10 yr of age"],"k":2,"ex":"Herpes zoster is the reactivation of latent VZV and shows no seasonal variation, unlike varicella which peaks in winter and spring.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Varicella is more severe in young infants (especially neonates).\n\n- **B.** Household transmission is about 65–86%.\n- **D.** Lifetime zoster risk after varicella is about 20–30%.\n- **E.** Zoster is rare in healthy children under 10 years.\n\nNelson explanation:\n121. (C). Herpes zoster is caused by the reactivation of latent VZV. It is not common in\nchildhood and shows no seasonal variation in incidence.\n\nHigh-yield:\nZoster = reactivation of latent VZV in a dorsal root ganglion; dermatomal vesicles.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 274 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":121,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-122","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Varicella is an acute febrile rash illness that is common in Iraq. Of the following, the TRUE statement is","o":["it usually begins 4-6 days after exposure","subclinical varicella is common","mild abdominal pain may occur 24-48 hr before the rash appears","temperature elevation usually as high as 41.1c","lesions often appear first on the extremities"],"k":2,"ex":"Mild abdominal pain and malaise can precede the varicella rash by 24–48 hours. The incubation period is 14–16 days (range 10–21).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Illness begins 14–16 days after exposure, not 4–6 days.\n\n- **B.** Subclinical varicella is rare.\n- **D.** Fever is usually 37.8–38.9 °C; 41.1 °C is the extreme, not usual.\n- **E.** Lesions typically start on the scalp, face or trunk.\n\nNelson explanation:\n122.(C). The illness usually begins 14-16 days after exposure, although the incubation\nperiod can range from 10-21 days. Subclinical varicella is rare. Temperature elevation is\nusually 37.8-38.9°C but may be as high as 41.1°C. Varicella lesions often appear first on\nthe scalp, face, or trunk.\n\nHigh-yield:\nVaricella: 14–21 day incubation (curriculum), centripetal pleomorphic pruritic vesicles in crops.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 274 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":122,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-123","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are true about varicella in unvaccinated individuals EXCEPT","o":["simultaneous presence of lesions in the same stages of evolution","distribution of the rash is predominantly central","many children have vesicular lesions on the eyelids","exanthem may be much more extensive in children with skin disorders","hypopigmentation or hyperpigmentation of lesion sites persists for days to weeks in some children"],"k":0,"ex":"Varicella lesions are in different stages at the same time (macule, papule, vesicle, crust), which is its hallmark. A statement of the same stage is therefore false.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** The rash is mainly central (trunk, face, scalp).\n\n- **C.** Eyelid vesicles are common.\n- **D.** The rash is more extensive in children with skin disorders such as eczema.\n- **E.** Pigment changes at lesion sites can last days to weeks.\n\nNelson explanation:\n123.(A). The simultaneous presence of lesions in various stages of evolution is\ncharacteristic of varicella.\n\nHigh-yield:\nVaricella rash is pleomorphic: lesions at different stages together (unlike smallpox).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 274 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":123,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-124","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Encephalitis and acute cerebellar ataxia are well-described neurologic complications of varicella. 264 [Continuation on source page 275] All the following are true EXCEPT younger than 5 yr and older than 20 yr","o":["meningoencephalitis","patients with cerebellar ataxia have a gradual onset of gait disturbance, nystagmus, and slurred speech","neurologic symptoms usually begin 2-6 days after the onset of the rash but may occur during the incubation period or after resolution of the rash","clinical recovery is typically occurring after 96 hr, and is usually gradual"],"k":null,"ex":"Neurological recovery in varicella cerebellitis/encephalitis is typically rapid (24–72 hours) and usually complete, so a recovery after 96 hours is the false statement.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Truncated in OCR: the age statement (\"younger than 5 yr and older than 20 yr\") refers to the age groups at risk of CNS complications.\n\n- **B.** Meningoencephalitis is a recognised complication.\n- **C.** Cerebellar ataxia has gradual onset of gait disturbance, nystagmus and slurred speech.\n- **D.** Neurological symptoms usually begin 2–6 days after the rash.\n\nNelson explanation:\n124.(E). Clinical recovery is typically rapid, occurring within 24-72 hr, and is usually\ncomplete.\n\nHigh-yield:\nPost-varicella cerebellitis: good prognosis, supportive treatment.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: the stem and option A are partly merged (\"EXCEPT younger than 5 yr and older than 20 yr\"), so option A cannot be quoted fully; the key (E) matches Nelson's explanation.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 274 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":124,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-125","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Oral therapy with acyclovir (20 mg/kg/dose; maximum: 800 mg/dose) given as 4 doses/day for 5 days can be used to treat uncomplicated varicella in individuals at increased risk for moderate to severe varicella. It includes all the following individuals ExCEPT","o":["nonpregnant individuals older than 12 yr of age","individuals older than 12 mo of age with chronic cutaneous disorders","individuals receiving short-term corticosteroid therapy","individuals receiving long-term salicylate therapy","individuals with chronic hematological disorders"],"k":4,"ex":"Oral acyclovir for uncomplicated varicella is used in people at higher risk: non-pregnant over 12 years, chronic skin or lung disease, long-term salicylates, or short-course corticosteroids. Chronic hematological disorders are not on this list.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Non-pregnant individuals over 12 years are included.\n\n- **B.** Children over 12 months with chronic skin disorders are included.\n- **C.** Short-term corticosteroid therapy is included.\n- **D.** Long-term salicylate therapy (Reye syndrome risk) is included.\n\nNelson explanation:\n125.(E).\n\nHigh-yield:\nAspirin must be avoided in varicella (Reye syndrome).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Curriculum teaches acyclovir mainly for immunodeficiency; the broader high-risk list is Nelson detail. Source answer (E) preserved.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 275 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":125,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-130","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Human herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6A and HHV-6B) and human herpesvirus 7 (HHV-7) cause infection in infancy and early childhood. HHV-6B is responsible for the majority of cases of roseola infantum (exanthema subitum or sixth disease). All the following are true ExcEPT","o":["95% of children being infected with HHV-6 by 2 yr of age","peak age of primary HHV-6B infection is 6-9 mo of life","congenital infection with HHV-6 occurs in 1% of newborns","congenital infection with HHV-7 is well recognized","breast milk does not play a role in transmission of either HHV-6 or HHV-7"],"k":3,"ex":"Congenital infection with HHV-7 has not been demonstrated. The other statements are true: HHV-6 infects about 95% of children by 2 years, peaks at 6–9 months, congenital HHV-6 is about 1%, and breast milk has no role.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** True: 95% are infected by 2 years.\n\n- **B.** True: peak age is 6–9 months.\n- **C.** True: congenital HHV-6 occurs in about 1% of newborns.\n- **E.** True: breast milk is not a transmission route.\n\nNelson explanation:\n130.(D). Congenital infection with HHV-7 has not been demonstrated.\n131.(0).\n\nHigh-yield:\nRoseola (HHV-6/7): 3 days of high fever, then rash as the fever falls; febrile convulsions.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 276 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":130,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-133","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"For military services, vaccines are available for human adenoviruses types","o":["1 and 4","2 and 5","3 and 6 primary infections in infants may manifest as bronchiolitis or pneumonia","4 and 7 pharyngitis typically includes symptoms of coryza, sore throat, and fever","5 and 8 (HAdV) infections in children and adults. 266 [Continuation on source page 277] All the following are true EXCEPT HAdV can be identified in <5% of children with isolated pharyngitis"],"k":3,"ex":"Adenovirus vaccines exist only for types 4 and 7 and are used only in military recruits.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Types 1 and 4 are not the vaccine pair.\n\n- **B.** Types 2 and 5 are common respiratory types without a vaccine.\n- **C.** Types 3 and 6 have no vaccine.\n- **E.** Types 5 and 8 have no vaccine (type 8 causes keratoconjunctivitis).\n\nNelson explanation:\n133.(D). Vaccines are available for HAdV types 4 and 7, but are used only for military\npopulations.\n\nHigh-yield:\nAdenovirus: pharyngoconjunctival fever, keratoconjunctivitis, haemorrhagic cystitis, gastroenteritis (types 40/41).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: the question block is merged with fragments of another adenovirus question (options B, D, E of a different stem); the key (D) matches Nelson's explanation.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 276 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":133,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-140","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following statements regarding giardiasis are true ExCEPT","o":["incubation period of Giardia infection usually is 1-2 wk","most infections are asymptomatic","symptomatic infections occur more frequently in adults than in children","stools do not contain blood, mucus, or fecal leukocytes","malabsorption of sugars, fats, and fat-soluble vitamins is well documented"],"k":2,"ex":"Symptomatic giardiasis is more common in children than in adults; most infections are asymptomatic, stools lack blood/mucus, and malabsorption is well documented.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** The incubation period is 1–2 weeks.\n\n- **B.** Most infections are asymptomatic.\n- **D.** Stools do not contain blood, mucus or leukocytes.\n- **E.** Malabsorption of sugars, fats and fat-soluble vitamins occurs.\n\nNelson explanation:\n140.(C).\n\nHigh-yield:\nGiardia: foul-smelling fatty stools, bloating, failure to thrive; diagnose by stool cysts/antigen.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 278 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":140,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-141","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Giardiasis should be considered in children who have the following presentations EXCEPT","o":["acute dysenteric diarrhea","persistent diarrhea","failure to thrive","malabsorption","chronic crampy abdominal pain"],"k":0,"ex":"Giardiasis causes non-dysenteric diarrhoea. Acute dysenteric (bloody) diarrhoea is not a giardiasis presentation.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Persistent diarrhoea is a classic presentation.\n\n- **C.** Failure to thrive is a recognised presentation.\n- **D.** Malabsorption is a recognised presentation.\n- **E.** Chronic crampy abdominal pain and bloating are recognised presentations.\n\nNelson explanation:\n141.(A). Giardiasis should be considered in children who have acute nondysenteric\ndiarrhea, persistent diarrhea, intermittent diarrhea and constipation, malabsorption,\nchronic crampy abdominal pain and bloating, failure to thrive, or weight loss.\n\nHigh-yield:\nBloody diarrhoea = shigella, amoeba, EHEC, campylobacter; not giardia.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 278 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":141,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-142","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Pregnant mother seek your advice about the stool examination of her asymptomatic 15 -month-old child which contain giardia cyst, your advice should be","o":["reassurance, no treatment","inidazole 50 mg\\kg once","nitazoxinide 200 mg bid for 3 days","Dmetronidazole15 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses for 5-7 days","albendazole 400 mg once a day for 5 days"],"k":3,"ex":"Asymptomatic carriers are usually not treated, but an exception is made when a toddler could transmit infection to a pregnant woman in the household. Metronidazole 15 mg/kg/day in 3 doses for 5–7 days is then used.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Reassurance alone is the general rule for asymptomatic carriers, but not when a pregnant mother is exposed.\n\n- **B.** Single-dose tinidazole 50 mg/kg is not licensed for a 15-month-old child.\n- **C.** Nitazoxanide 200 mg twice daily is the dose for ages 4–11 years, not for a 15-month-old.\n- **E.** Albendazole is an alternative agent, not the preferred choice.\n\nNelson explanation:\n142.(D). Asymptomatic excreters generally are not treated except in specific instances\nsuch as outbreak control, prevention of household transmission by toddlers to\npregnant women and patients with hypogammaglobulinemia or cystic fibrosis, and\nsituations requiring oral antibiotic treatment where Giardia may produce\nmalabsorption of the antibiotic.Tinidazole not recommended below 3 years old as\nalbendazole not recommended below age of 6 years. Nitazoxanide1-3 yr: 100 mg bid\nfor 3 days\n\nHigh-yield:\nGiardia: treat symptomatic cases (metronidazole/tinidazole/nitazoxanide); treat carriers only in special situations.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: options C and D are merged in the transcription; the key (D) corresponds to the metronidazole option.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 278 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":142,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-143","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The largest protozoan that parasitizes human is","o":["Entamoeba histolytica","Giardia lamblia","Balantidium coli","Isospora belli","Cryptosporidium"],"k":2,"ex":"Balantidium coli is the largest protozoan parasite of humans.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Entamoeba histolytica is smaller.\n\n- **B.** Giardia lamblia is smaller.\n- **D.** Isospora belli is smaller.\n- **E.** Cryptosporidium is among the smallest.\n\nNelson explanation:\n143.(C).\n\nHigh-yield:\nBalantidium coli: pig reservoir; causes dysentery-like colitis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 278 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":143,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-016","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications","q":"The incidence of all the following are increased in large for gestational age newborn EXCEPT","o":["cephalohematoma",". hypocalcemia","hypoglycemia","congenital heart disease","developmental retardation"],"k":1,"ex":"LGA infants have more birth injuries (cephalohematoma, brachial plexus, clavicle fracture), hypoglycemia, polycythemia, congenital heart disease and developmental problems; hypocalcemia is not listed as increased in LGA infants as a group in the source.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Cephalohematoma: increased.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Hypoglycemia: increased.\n- **D.** Congenital heart disease: increased.\n- **E.** Developmental retardation: increased.\n\nHigh-yield:\nHypocalcemia is common in infants of diabetic mothers, a subset of LGA infants.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q16, PDF p.165; answer on PDF p.180","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":16,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-021","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Regarding cephalohematoma, all the following are true ExCEPT","o":["is a subperiosteal hemorrhage","it may extend across the midline and across suture lines","occur in 1-2% of live births","no discoloration of the overlying scalp","an underlying skull fracture may be associated with 10-25% of cases"],"k":1,"ex":"A cephalohematoma is subperiosteal, so it is limited by suture lines and does not cross the midline. A caput succedaneum is the swelling that crosses sutures.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Subperiosteal hemorrhage: true.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Occurs in 1-2% of live births: true.\n- **D.** No discoloration of the overlying scalp: true.\n- **E.** Underlying skull fracture in 10-25%: true.\n\nHigh-yield:\nCephalohematoma can contribute to neonatal jaundice.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q21, PDF p.166; answer on PDF p.180","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":21,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-024","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The following are true regarding apnea ExCEPT","o":["apnea is a common problem in preterm infants","in term infants, apnea is always worrisome","obstructive apnea is characterized by absence of airflow but persistent chest wall motion","serious apnea is defined as cessation of breathing for longer than 20 sec","bradycardia follows the apnea by 1-2 sec in more than 95% of cases and is most often nodal"],"k":4,"ex":"Bradycardia follows apnea by 1-2 seconds in over 95% of cases and is most often sinus, occasionally nodal, so calling it most often nodal is false.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Common in preterm infants: true.\n\n- **B.** In term infants apnea is always worrisome: true.\n- **C.** Obstructive apnea: no airflow with persistent chest wall motion, true.\n- **D.** Serious apnea is more than 20 seconds: true.\n- **E.** Correct (source key).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q24, PDF p.167; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":24,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-025","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The risk for development of respiratory distress syndrome RDS increases with the following conditions EXCEPT","o":["prolonged rupture of membranes","maternal diabetes","multiple births",".precipitous delivery","cold stress"],"k":0,"ex":"Prolonged rupture of membranes lowers the risk of RDS by accelerating lung maturation. Maternal diabetes, multiple births, precipitous delivery and cold stress raise it.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Correct (source key).\n\n- **B.** Maternal diabetes: increases risk.\n- **C.** Multiple births: increases risk.\n- **D.** Precipitous delivery: increases risk (label lost in OCR).\n- **E.** Cold stress: increases risk.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q25, PDF p.167; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":25,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-029","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The basic defect requiring treatment in RDS is","o":["metabolic acidosis","circulatory insufficiency","hypothermia","inadequate pulmonary exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide","electrolytes disturbance"],"k":3,"ex":"RDS is surfactant deficiency causing alveolar collapse and inadequate gas exchange (hypoxemia and hypercapnia). Acidosis and circulatory insufficiency are secondary.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Metabolic acidosis: secondary.\n\n- **B.** Circulatory insufficiency: secondary.\n- **C.** Hypothermia: aggravating factor.\n- **D.** Correct (source key).\n- **E.** Electrolyte disturbance: secondary.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q29, PDF p.168; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":29,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-030","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Regarding CPAP, the following are true EXCEPT","o":["its prevents collapse of surfactant-deficient alveoli","its indicated, if oxygen saturation cannot be kept > 95%","its improves functional residual capacity FRC","its improves ventilation-perfusion matching","its reduces ventilatory needs"],"k":1,"ex":"CPAP is indicated when oxygen saturation cannot be kept above 90% at FiO2 of 40-70%, not above 95%; option B is false. CPAP prevents alveolar collapse, raises FRC, improves ventilation-perfusion matching and reduces ventilatory needs.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Prevents collapse of surfactant-deficient alveoli: true.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Improves FRC: true.\n- **D.** Improves V/Q matching: true.\n- **E.** Reduces ventilatory needs: true.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q30, PDF p.168; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":30,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-031","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Treatment","q":"Prophylactic and rescue administrations of synthetic and natural surfactants have the following advantages EXCEPT","o":["reduces adverse outcomes","reduces neonatal mortality","decreses the risk for pneumothorax","decreses the risk for pulmonary interstitial emphysema","reduces bronco-pulmonary dysplasia BPD rates"],"k":4,"ex":"Surfactant lowers mortality, pneumothorax and pulmonary interstitial emphysema. It has not lowered BPD rates, probably because very sick infants who would have died now survive.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Reduces adverse outcomes: true.\n\n- **B.** Reduces neonatal mortality: true.\n- **C.** Decreases pneumothorax: true.\n- **D.** Decreases PIE: true.\n- **E.** Correct (source key).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q31, PDF p.168; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":31,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-036","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"One of the foilowing is FALsE in the chest radiograph of transient tachypnea of the newborn (TTN)","o":["prominent pulmonary vascular markings","fluid in the intralobar fissures","overaeration",".flat diaphragms","diffuse reticulogranular pattern"],"k":4,"ex":"TTN shows prominent vascular markings, fluid in the fissures, overaeration and flat diaphragms. A diffuse reticulogranular pattern with air bronchograms and hypoaeration is RDS.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Prominent vascular markings: true.\n\n- **B.** Fluid in the fissures: true.\n- **C.** Overaeration: true.\n- **D.** Flat diaphragms: true.\n- **E.** Correct (source key).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q36, PDF p.169; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":36,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-037","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The following are true regarding meconium aspiration syndrome (MAS) ExCEPT","o":["(MAS) develops in 5% of meconium-stained infants",".30% require mechanical ventilation","3-5% die","overdistention of the chest may be prominent","usually occurs in preterm or near-term infants"],"k":4,"ex":"MAS occurs in term and post-term infants, not preterm infants. It develops in about 5% of meconium-stained infants, 30% need ventilation and 3-5% die.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** 5% of stained infants: true.\n\n- **B.** 30% need ventilation: true (label lost in OCR).\n- **C.** 3-5% die: true.\n- **D.** Chest overdistention: true.\n- **E.** Correct (source key).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q37, PDF p.169; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":37,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-040","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) can be diagnosed on prenatal ultrasonography (between 16 and 24 wk of gestation) in > 50% of cases. Findings on ultrasonography may include the following ExCEPT","o":["chest mass","mediastinal shift","gastric bubble","olighydramnios","a liver in the thoracic cavity"],"k":3,"ex":"CDH is associated with polyhydramnios; oligohydramnios is not a finding. Antenatal ultrasound shows a chest mass, mediastinal shift, intrathoracic stomach bubble and liver in the chest.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Chest mass: finding.\n\n- **B.** Mediastinal shift: finding.\n- **C.** Gastric bubble in the chest: finding.\n- **D.** Correct (source key).\n- **E.** Liver in the thorax: finding.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q40, PDF p.170; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":40,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-043","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"All the following are true, regarding pathology of neonatal necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) EXCEPT","o":["in fatal cases, gangrene may extend from the stomach to the rectum","the greatest risk factor for NEC is prematurity","in most situations, a pathogen is identified","NEC is much less common in infants fed human milk ENEC in term infants is often a \"secondary\" disease"],"k":2,"ex":"In most NEC cases no pathogen is isolated, so the statement that a pathogen is identified in most is false.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Gangrene from stomach to rectum in fatal cases: true.\n\n- **B.** Prematurity is the greatest risk factor: true.\n- **C.** Correct (source key).\n- **D.** Less common with human milk: true.\n- **E.** Term NEC is often secondary: true.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q43, PDF p.170; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":43,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-047","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"A search to determine the cause of jaundice should be made in all the following conditions EXCEPT","o":["if it appears in the first 24-36 hr of life","if serum bilirubin is rising at a rate faster than 5 mg/dL/24 hr","if serum bilirubin is >12 mg/dl in a full-term infant","if jaundice persists after 10-14 days of life","if direct bilirubin fraction is >1 mg/dL at any time"],"k":4,"ex":"The source threshold for investigating a direct bilirubin is above 2 mg/dL at any time, not 1 mg/dL. The other items are standard triggers for a jaundice work-up.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Onset in the first 24-36 h: search.\n\n- **B.** Rise faster than 5 mg/dL/24 h: search.\n- **C.** Bilirubin above 12 mg/dL in a term infant: search.\n- **D.** Persistence beyond 10-14 days: search.\n- **E.** Correct (source key).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q47, PDF p.171; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":47,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-048","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"48 Regarding breast milk jaundice, the following are true ExCEPT","o":["it develops in an estimated 2% of breast-fed term infants","maximal unconjugated bilirubin concentrations as high as 10-30 mg/dl reached during the 2nd-3rd week","jaundice may persist for 3-10 wk phototherapy may be of benefit","kernicterus never occur"],"k":null,"ex":"Kernicterus can occur, although rarely, in breast milk jaundice, so the statement that it never occurs is false.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** About 2% of breast-fed term infants: true.\n\n- **B.** Peaks of 10-30 mg/dL at 2-3 weeks: true.\n- **C.** May persist 3-10 weeks: true.\n- **D.** Phototherapy may help: true.\n- **E.** Correct (source key).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q48, PDF p.171; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":48,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-052","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"One of the following is FALsE in hemolytic disease of the newborn caused by blood group A and B incompatibility","o":["ABO incompatibility occurs in 20-25% of pregnancies","A is more antigenic than A2","natural antibodies against A and B factors are usually IgM antibodies that do not cross the placenta","isoimmune hemolytic disease may be found in first-born infants irrespective of infant blood group","most cases are mild"],"k":3,"ex":"ABO hemolytic disease can occur in a first-born infant because maternal IgG anti-A/B may already be present, but only when the infant's blood group carries the antigen; it is not irrespective of infant blood group.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** ABO incompatibility in 20-25% of pregnancies: true.\n\n- **B.** A1 more antigenic than A2: true.\n- **C.** Natural antibodies are IgM and do not cross: true.\n- **D.** Correct (source key).\n- **E.** Most cases are mild: true.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q52, PDF p.172; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":52,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-053","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"One of the following is FALSE in hemolytic disease of the newborn caused by Rh incompatibility","o":["infant is Rh Positive (D, sometimes C)","never occur in first-born infant","maternal antibody titers can help predict the severity of fetal disease","severe anemia is frequent","stillbirth/hydrops is frequent"],"k":1,"ex":"Rh disease can affect first-born infants (about 5%), so the statement that it never occurs in the first-born is false.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Infant Rh positive: true.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Maternal antibody titers predict severity: true.\n- **D.** Severe anemia is frequent: true.\n- **E.** Stillbirth or hydrops is frequent: true.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q53, PDF p.172; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":53,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-058","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Surgical repair of umbilical hernia is advised in the following conditions ExCEPT","o":["if hernia persists to the age of 4-5 yr","causes symptoms","hernias that appear before the age of 6 mo","becomes strangulated","becomes progressively larger after the age of 1-2 yr"],"k":2,"ex":"Most umbilical hernias that appear before 6 months resolve spontaneously, so early appearance is not an indication for surgery. Repair is for persistence to 4-5 years, symptoms, strangulation or progressive enlargement after 1-2 years.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Persistence to 4-5 years: indication.\n\n- **B.** Symptoms: indication.\n- **C.** Correct (source key).\n- **D.** Strangulation: indication.\n- **E.** Progressive enlargement after 1-2 years: indication.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q58, PDF p.173; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":58,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-061","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"In Infants of diabetic mothers, the following are true EXCEPT","o":["hypoglycemia develops in about 25-50% of infants of diabetic mothers increased weight of the placenta and all infant organs","heart failure occurs in 5-10% of infants of diabetic mothers","the incidence of congenital anomalies is increased threefold in infants of diabetic mothers","lumbosacral agenesis is one of most common congenital anomalies"],"k":1,"ex":"In infants of diabetic mothers the placenta and organs are enlarged except the brain, so \"all infant organs\" is false. Maternal hyperglycemia leads to fetal hyperinsulinism.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Hypoglycemia in 25-50%: true.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Heart failure in 5-10%: true.\n- **D.** Anomalies increased threefold: true.\n- **E.** Lumbosacral agenesis is among the commonest anomalies: true.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q61, PDF p.174; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":61,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-065","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (TORCH)","q":"include the following EXCEPT 65. Intrauterine transplacental infections of significance to the fetus and/or newborn","o":["hepatitis B virus (HBV)","rubella",". toxoplasmosis","Parvovirus B19","varicella"],"k":0,"ex":"Hepatitis B, HIV, HSV and TB are mainly transmitted intrapartum or postnatally, whereas rubella, toxoplasmosis, parvovirus B19 and varicella are classic transplacental infections.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Correct (source key).\n\n- **B.** Rubella: transplacental.\n- **C.** Toxoplasmosis: transplacental.\n- **D.** Parvovirus B19: transplacental.\n- **E.** Varicella: transplacental.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q65, PDF pp.174-175; answer on PDF p.184","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":65,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-067","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Prevention","q":"One of the following is FALSE regarding intrapartum antibiotics","o":["reduce vertical transmission of GBS","lessen neonatal morbidity after preterm rupture of membranes","prevent perinatal transmission of GBS","reduce the rates of late-onset G8S disease","has no effect on the rates of infection with non-GBs pathogens"],"k":3,"ex":"Intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis reduces early-onset GBS disease but not late-onset disease, and does not affect non-GBS infection.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Reduces vertical transmission: true.\n\n- **B.** Lessens morbidity after PPROM: true (label lost in OCR).\n- **C.** Prevents perinatal GBS transmission: true.\n- **D.** Correct (source key).\n- **E.** No effect on non-GBS infection: true.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q67, PDF p.175; answer on PDF p.184","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":67,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-069","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Osteitis may be a feature of one of the following transplacental infections","o":["cytomegalovirus","herpes simplex virus","varicella-zoster virus","rubella","T. gondlii"],"k":3,"ex":"Osteitis is a feature of congenital rubella and congenital syphilis, so rubella is the correct option.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** CMV: not a feature.\n\n- **B.** HSV: not a feature.\n- **C.** VZV: not a feature.\n- **D.** Correct (source key).\n- **E.** Toxoplasmosis: not a feature.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q69, PDF p.175; answer on PDF p.184","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":69,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-070","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Intracranial calcification may be a feature of one of the following transplacental infections","o":["cytomegalovirus",".herpes simplex virus","varicella-zoster virus","syphilis","rubella"],"k":0,"ex":"Intracranial calcification in the source list is a feature of CMV (also HIV, toxoplasmosis, T. cruzi). Only CMV is among the options in that list.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Correct (source key).\n\n- **B.** HSV: not in the source list.\n- **C.** VZV: not in the source list.\n- **D.** Syphilis: not in the source list.\n- **E.** Rubella: not in the source list.\n\nHigh-yield:\nPeriventricular calcification suggests CMV; diffuse calcification suggests toxoplasmosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q70, PDF p.175; answer on PDF p.184","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":70,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-079","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The following factors suggest hemolytic disease as a cause of jaundice in the newborn EXCEPT","o":["bilirubin rise of >0.5 mg/dL/h","reticulocytosis >5% at birth","onset of jaundice before 24 hours of age","significant decrease in hemoglobin","failure of phototherapy to lower serum bilirubin levels"],"k":1,"ex":"Reticulocytosis suggesting hemolysis is above 8% at birth (above 5% on days 2-3, above 2% after the first week); above 5% at birth is therefore not the stated criterion.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Rise above 0.5 mg/dL/h: suggests hemolysis.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Onset before 24 h: suggests hemolysis.\n- **D.** Significant Hb drop: suggests hemolysis.\n- **E.** Failure of phototherapy: suggests hemolysis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q79, PDF p.177; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":79,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-001","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (Epidemiology)","q":"Major cause of neonatal mortality in full-term newborn is","o":["respiratory distress syndrome","necrotizing enterocolitis","bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD)","congenital anomalies","intraventricular hemorrhage"],"k":3,"ex":"Congenital anomalies are the leading cause of neonatal death in full-term newborns. The immature-lung and gut diseases (RDS, NEC, BPD, IVH) are problems of preterm infants, which is where Nelson places them as major causes of mortality.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** RDS (surfactant deficiency) is mainly a preterm disease; it drives preterm, not term, mortality.\n\n- **B.** NEC is predominantly a disease of preterm infants.\n- **C.** BPD is a chronic complication of prematurity and ventilation.\n- **D.** Correct (source key).\n- **E.** IVH is mainly seen in preterm infants.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q1, PDF p.163; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":1,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-002","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Generalized edema of the newborn may occur in the following conditions ExCEPT","o":["prematurity","nonimmune hydrops","Turner syndrome",". congenital nephrosis","Hurler syndrome"],"k":2,"ex":"Turner syndrome causes localized lymphedema (typically hands and feet) rather than generalized edema, so it is the exception. Generalized edema of the newborn occurs with prematurity, nonimmune hydrops, congenital nephrosis (hypoalbuminemia) and storage disease such as Hurler syndrome.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Prematurity: immature capillary/renal handling and low oncotic pressure give generalized edema.\n\n- **B.** Nonimmune hydrops is generalized fluid accumulation by definition.\n- **C.** Correct (source key): Turner gives localized extremity lymphedema.\n- **D.** Congenital nephrosis: massive protein loss, generalized edema (label D lost in OCR; restored by sequence).\n- **E.** Hurler syndrome can present with hydrops/generalized edema.\n\nHigh-yield:\nEdema limited to the extremities of a female infant can be the first sign of Turner syndrome.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q2, PDF p.163; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":2,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-003","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Soft areas in the occipital region suggest the irregular calcification and wormian bone formation usually associated with the following conditions ExCEPT","o":["osteogenesis imperfecta","craniosynostosis","cleidocranial dysostosis","cretinism","Down syndrome"],"k":1,"ex":"Craniosynostosis is premature suture fusion, giving small or absent fontanels and a ridged skull; it does not produce soft occipital areas or wormian bones. The other conditions delay ossification and are linked with wormian bones and soft areas.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective bone, wormian bones.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key): sutures fuse early, so fontanels are small/closed.\n- **C.** Cleidocranial dysostosis: delayed ossification, wormian bones.\n- **D.** Cretinism (congenital hypothyroidism): delayed bone age, wide fontanels.\n- **E.** Down syndrome: delayed ossification, wide fontanels/sutures.\n\nHigh-yield:\nPersistently small fontanels: microcephaly, craniosynostosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q3, PDF p.163; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":3,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-004","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"The following disorders are associated with a large anterior fontanel ExCEpT","o":["congenital rubella syndrome","hypophosphatasia","Apert syndrome","Russell-Silver syndrome","vitamin A deficiency"],"k":4,"ex":"Vitamin A deficiency is not a recognized cause of a large anterior fontanel; the vitamin deficiency linked with it is vitamin D (rickets). Congenital rubella, hypophosphatasia, Apert syndrome and Russell-Silver syndrome are all associated with a large anterior fontanel.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Congenital rubella: associated with a large fontanel.\n\n- **B.** Hypophosphatasia: defective mineralization, wide fontanel.\n- **C.** Apert syndrome: associated with a large fontanel despite craniosynostosis.\n- **D.** Russell-Silver syndrome: associated with a large fontanel (label D lost in OCR; restored by sequence).\n- **E.** Correct (source key): not a recognized cause.\n\nHigh-yield:\nOther causes of a large fontanel include hypothyroidism, hydrocephalus and rickets.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q4, PDF p.163; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":4,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-006","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"A single umbilical artery in a newborn infant increases the risk for","o":["meningomyelocele","occult renal anomaly","omphalocele","gastroschisis","omphalitis"],"k":1,"ex":"A single umbilical artery (two-vessel cord) raises the risk of an occult (often urinary tract) renal anomaly, so renal ultrasound is considered.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Meningomyelocele is not linked to a single umbilical artery in the source.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Omphalocele: abdominal wall defect, not the recognized association here.\n- **D.** Gastroschisis: not the recognized association.\n- **E.** Omphalitis is an infection of the cord stump, unrelated to the vessel count.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q6, PDF p.164; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":6,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-008","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Of the following, the condition which is associated with polyhydramnios is","o":["renal agenesis (Potter syndrome)","Prune-belly syndrome","pulmonary hypoplasia","intestinal pseudo-obstruction","diaphragmatic hernia"],"k":4,"ex":"Diaphragmatic hernia is associated with polyhydramnios (impaired fetal swallowing); the other listed conditions are associated with oligohydramnios, which is the contrast Nelson draws.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Renal agenesis (Potter): no fetal urine, oligohydramnios.\n\n- **B.** Prune-belly syndrome: oligohydramnios (poor urine output).\n- **C.** Pulmonary hypoplasia: a consequence/cause of oligohydramnios.\n- **D.** Intestinal pseudo-obstruction: oligohydramnios per the source.\n- **E.** Correct (source key).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q8, PDF p.164; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":8,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-009","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Low maternal serum α-fetoprotein (MSAFP) is associated with","o":["open neural tube defects","trisomy 21","gastroschisis","omphalocele","congenital nephrosis"],"k":1,"ex":"Low maternal serum AFP is associated with trisomy 21 (and 18), wrong dating and intrauterine growth restriction. Open neural tube defects, gastroschisis, omphalocele and congenital nephrosis raise MSAFP.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Open NTD: high MSAFP (about 90% detected).\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key): trisomy 21 gives low MSAFP.\n- **C.** Gastroschisis: high MSAFP.\n- **D.** Omphalocele: high MSAFP.\n- **E.** Congenital nephrosis: high MSAFP.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q9, PDF p.164; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":9,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-010","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"A delay in fetal pulmonary maturation may be associated with","o":["hydrops fetalis","severe premature separation of the placenta","premature rupture of the fetal membranes","narcotic addiction","maternal hypertensive and renal vascular disease"],"k":0,"ex":"Hydrops fetalis (and maternal diabetes without vascular disease) delays pulmonary maturation. Chronic intrauterine stress (abruption, PROM, narcotic addiction, maternal hypertension/renal vascular disease) accelerates lung maturation.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Correct (source key).\n\n- **B.** Severe placental abruption: stress accelerates maturation.\n- **C.** PROM: accelerates maturation.\n- **D.** Narcotic addiction: accelerates maturation.\n- **E.** Maternal hypertensive/renal vascular disease: accelerates maturation.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q10, PDF p.164; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":10,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-014","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"One of the following drugs may cause pyloric stenosis if administered to a premature infant","o":["intravenous vitamin E","indomethacin","enteric gentamicin",". prostaglandins","dexamethasone"],"k":3,"ex":"Prostaglandins (used to keep the ductus open) and erythromycin are the drugs Nelson links with pyloric stenosis in premature infants.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** IV vitamin E: not linked with pyloric stenosis in the source.\n\n- **B.** Indomethacin: renal impairment, bowel perforation; not pyloric stenosis.\n- **C.** Enteral gentamicin: not linked.\n- **D.** Correct (source key; label D lost in OCR, restored by sequence).\n- **E.** Dexamethasone: risks include cerebral palsy; not linked with pyloric stenosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q14, PDF p.165; answer on PDF p.180","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":14,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-015","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications","q":"The following factors pose a risk for poor academic performance of premature infants EXCEPT","o":["birthweight below 750 g","periventricular leukomalacia","antenatal exposure to magnesium sulfate","bronchopulmonary dysplasia","posthemorrhagic hydrocephalus"],"k":2,"ex":"Antenatal magnesium sulfate is neuroprotective and lowers cerebral palsy, so it is not a risk factor for poor school performance. Extremely low birth weight, periventricular leukomalacia, BPD and posthemorrhagic hydrocephalus are.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Birth weight below 750 g: risk factor.\n\n- **B.** PVL: risk factor.\n- **C.** Correct (source key).\n- **D.** BPD: risk factor.\n- **E.** Posthemorrhagic hydrocephalus: risk factor.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q15, PDF p.165; answer on PDF p.180","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":15,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-020","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Vomiting in the neonatal period is usually due to","o":["pyloric stenosis","milk allergy","overfeeding","stress ulcer","an inborn error of metabolism"],"k":2,"ex":"Overfeeding, poor feeding technique and normal reflux are the commonest causes of neonatal vomiting. Pathological causes (obstruction, raised intracranial pressure, sepsis, metabolic disease, adrenal insufficiency) are rarer but must be excluded, especially with day-1 or bilious vomiting.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Pyloric stenosis: typically presents in weeks 2-8, rare in the neonatal period.\n\n- **B.** Milk allergy: uncommon cause.\n- **C.** Correct (source key).\n- **D.** Stress ulcer: uncommon.\n- **E.** Inborn error of metabolism: uncommon, though vital to recognize.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q20, PDF p.166; answer on PDF p.180","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":20,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-032","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Vitamin A supplementation given largely to infants < 1,000 g resulted in all the following EXCEPT","o":["a decrease in death","a decrease in bronco-pulmonary dysplasia BPD at 36 wk","less nosocomial sepsis","less retinopathy of prematurity","decreases the need for extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO)"],"k":4,"ex":"Of the outcomes listed, the exception is the reduced need for ECMO: that effect belongs to inhaled nitric oxide in term/near-term infants with hypoxic respiratory failure or PPHN, not to vitamin A. Vitamin A supplementation (5,000 IU IM three times weekly for 4 weeks) is used to reduce BPD in VLBW infants.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Decrease in death: counted among vitamin A benefits in the stem list.\n\n- **B.** Decrease in BPD at 36 weeks: the main benefit.\n- **C.** Less nosocomial sepsis: listed in the stem as a result.\n- **D.** Less ROP: listed in the stem as a result.\n- **E.** Correct (source key): this is an iNO effect (label E lost in OCR, restored by sequence).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q32, PDF p.168; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":32,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-035","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Methylxanthines for the treatment of broncho-pulmonary dysplasia (BPD) have the following effects EXCEPT","o":["increase respiratory drive","decrease apnea","improve diaphragmatic contractility","increase pulmonary vascular resistance PVR","diuretic effects"],"k":3,"ex":"Methylxanthines decrease (not increase) pulmonary vascular resistance and increase lung compliance through smooth-muscle relaxation. They also raise respiratory drive, reduce apnea, improve diaphragmatic contractility and are diuretic.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Increase respiratory drive: true.\n\n- **B.** Decrease apnea: true.\n- **C.** Improve diaphragmatic contractility: true.\n- **D.** Correct (source key): they decrease PVR.\n- **E.** Diuretic effect: true.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q35, PDF p.169; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":35,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-041","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (Prognosis)","q":"A poor prognostic sign of congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) is","o":["grunting","use of accessory muscles","early respiratory distress, within 6 hr of life","cyanosis","scaphoid abdomen"],"k":2,"ex":"In congenital diaphragmatic hernia, respiratory distress within the first 6 hours of life signals severe pulmonary hypoplasia and is a poor prognostic sign. Grunting, accessory-muscle use and cyanosis are presenting signs and scaphoid abdomen is a diagnostic clue.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Grunting: a sign of distress, not specifically prognostic.\n\n- **B.** Accessory muscle use: same.\n- **C.** Correct (source key).\n- **D.** Cyanosis: a presenting sign.\n- **E.** Scaphoid abdomen: classic physical finding (bowel in chest).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q41, PDF p.170; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":41,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-042","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Eventration of the diaphragm may be associated with the following ExCEPT","o":["pulmonary hypoplasia","pulmonary sequestration","congenital heart disease","chromosomal trisomies","recurrent infections"],"k":0,"ex":"Per Nelson, eventration may affect lung development but is not associated with pulmonary hypoplasia. Pulmonary sequestration, congenital heart disease, chromosomal trisomies and recurrent chest infections are associated.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Correct (source key).\n\n- **B.** Pulmonary sequestration: associated.\n- **C.** Congenital heart disease: associated.\n- **D.** Chromosomal trisomies: associated.\n- **E.** Recurrent infections: associated.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q42, PDF p.170; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":42,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-051","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"The blood smear of infant with hemolytic disease of the newborn (erythroblastosis fetalis) typically shows the following ExCEPT","o":["polychromasia","increasedreticulocyte count","a marked increase in nucleated RBCs","thrombocytopenia","low white blood cell count"],"k":4,"ex":"In hemolytic disease of the newborn the blood smear shows polychromasia, high reticulocytes, many nucleated red cells and often thrombocytopenia; the white blood cell count is usually normal and may be raised, so a low WBC count is the exception.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Polychromasia: typical.\n\n- **B.** Increased reticulocytes: typical.\n- **C.** Marked increase in nucleated RBCs: typical.\n- **D.** Thrombocytopenia: may occur.\n- **E.** Correct (source key).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q51, PDF p.172; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":51,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-056","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"Apt test is useful for diagnosis of Adisseminated intravascular coagulopathy","o":["swallowed blood syndrome","hemorrhagic disease of the newborn","neonatal thrombocytopenic purpura","congenital deficiency of factor VII"],"k":1,"ex":"The Apt test distinguishes fetal from maternal blood in blood-stained stool or vomit: fetal hemoglobin resists alkali (stays pink) whereas maternal hemoglobin turns yellow-brown. It therefore diagnoses swallowed maternal blood syndrome.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** DIC: diagnosed by platelets and coagulation studies.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn: vitamin K deficiency, PT/PTT.\n- **D.** Neonatal thrombocytopenic purpura: platelet count.\n- **E.** Factor VII deficiency: factor assay.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q56, PDF p.173; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":56,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-057","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The normal cord at term is 55 cm long. Abnormally short cords are associated with the following EXCEPT","o":["fetal hypotonia","wrapping around fetal parts","uterine constraint","oligohydramnios","increased risk for complications of labor and delivery for both mother and infant"],"k":1,"ex":"Wrapping around fetal parts is a feature of long cords (more than 70 cm), which also cause true knots and prolapse. Short cords are associated with hypotonia, uterine constraint, oligohydramnios and labor complications.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Fetal hypotonia: associated with short cords.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key): this goes with long cords.\n- **C.** Uterine constraint: associated.\n- **D.** Oligohydramnios: associated.\n- **E.** Labor and delivery complications: associated.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q57, PDF p.173; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":57,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-059","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Generalized edema may be seen in the neonatal period with the following conditions EXCEPT","o":["congenital nephrosis","Turner syndrome","Hurler syndrome",".hydrops fetalis","offspring of diabetic mothers"],"k":1,"ex":"Turner syndrome gives persistent localized edema of the extremities, not generalized edema. Congenital nephrosis, Hurler syndrome, hydrops fetalis and offspring of diabetic mothers can show generalized edema.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Congenital nephrosis: generalized edema.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Hurler syndrome: generalized edema.\n- **D.** Hydrops fetalis: generalized edema (label lost in OCR).\n- **E.** Offspring of diabetic mothers: may show generalized edema.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q59, PDF p.173; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":59,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-060","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications","q":"Diabetic mothers have a high incidence of all the following EXCEPT","o":["oligohydramnios","preeclampsia","pyelonephritis",". preterm labor","chronic hypertension"],"k":0,"ex":"Diabetic mothers have polyhydramnios, not oligohydramnios. They also have a high incidence of preeclampsia, pyelonephritis, preterm labor and chronic hypertension.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Correct (source key).\n\n- **B.** Preeclampsia: increased.\n- **C.** Pyelonephritis: increased.\n- **D.** Preterm labor: increased.\n- **E.** Chronic hypertension: increased.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q60, PDF pp.173-174; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":60,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-062","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (Dysmorphology)","q":"The following definitions are true ExCEPT Aa syndrome is defined as a pattern of multiple abnormalities that are related by pathophysiology and result from a common defined etiology","o":["dysplasia sequence is a poor organization of cells into tissues or organs","an association refers to a nonrandom collection of malformations in which there is an unclear relationship among the malformations","disruption sequence is a mechanical (uterine) forces that alter structure of intrinsically normal tissue","malformation sequence is a single, local tissue morphogenesis abnormality that produces a chain of subsequent defects"],"k":3,"ex":"The description in option D is a deformation sequence (mechanical uterine forces on normal tissue). A disruption sequence is tissue destruction after a period of normal morphogenesis.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Syndrome definition: true.\n\n- **B.** Dysplasia: poor organization of cells into tissues, true.\n- **C.** Association: nonrandom malformations without clear pathogenesis, true.\n- **D.** Correct (source key): describes deformation.\n- **E.** Malformation sequence: true.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q62, PDF p.174; answer on PDF p.184","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":62,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-063","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (Genetics)","q":"Chromosomal causes of congenital malformations include","o":["Prader-Willi syndrome","X-linked hydrocephalus","Achondroplasia","Treacher Collins syndrome","Apert disease"],"k":0,"ex":"Prader-Willi syndrome is the chromosomal (15q11-q13 imprinting/deletion) cause in the list. X-linked hydrocephalus, achondroplasia, Treacher Collins syndrome and Apert disease are monogenic causes per the source.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Correct (source key).\n\n- **B.** X-linked hydrocephalus: monogenic.\n- **C.** Achondroplasia: monogenic.\n- **D.** Treacher Collins: monogenic.\n- **E.** Apert: monogenic.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q63, PDF p.174; answer on PDF p.184","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":63,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-064","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (Dysmorphology)","q":"One of the following definitions is FALSE","o":["brachydactyly=A condition of having short digits","postaxial polydactyly=Extra finger or toe present on the medial side of the hand or foot","clinodactyly=A medial or lateral curving of the fingers","camptodactyly=Permanent flexion of one or more fingers","syndactyly=Incomplete separation of the fingers"],"k":1,"ex":"Postaxial polydactyly is an extra digit on the lateral (ulnar/fibular) side; the medial side is preaxial. Option B is therefore false.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Brachydactyly: short digits, true.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key): medial is preaxial.\n- **C.** Clinodactyly: true.\n- **D.** Camptodactyly: true.\n- **E.** Syndactyly: true.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q64, PDF p.174; answer on PDF p.184","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":64,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-068","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (Microbiology)","q":"In a cohort of 6,215 VLBW infants in the NiCHD Neonatal Research Network, gram- positive agents were associated with which percent of cases of late-onset sepsis","o":["90%","70%","50%","30%","10%"],"k":1,"ex":"In the NICHD cohort of 6,215 VLBW infants, gram-positive organisms caused 70% of late-onset sepsis, gram-negative 18% and fungi 12%.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** 90%: too high.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** 50%: too low.\n- **D.** 30%: too low.\n- **E.** 10%: too low.\n\nHigh-yield:\nCoagulase-negative staphylococci dominate late-onset sepsis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q68, PDF p.175; answer on PDF p.184","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":68,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-072","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Common respiratory tract manifestations of neonatal bacterlal infections is","o":["ethmoiditis",".otitis media","mastoiditis","retropharyngeal ceflulitis","empyema"],"k":4,"ex":"Pneumonia and empyema are the common respiratory tract manifestations of neonatal bacterial infection; ear, sinus and retropharyngeal infections are uncommon in newborns.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Ethmoiditis: rare.\n\n- **B.** Otitis media: not a common manifestation here.\n- **C.** Mastoiditis: rare.\n- **D.** Retropharyngeal cellulitis: rare.\n- **E.** Correct (source key).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q72, PDF p.176; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":72,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-075","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (Prognosis)","q":"Neonatal sepsis case fatality rate is highest for","o":["Staphylococcus—coagulase negative","Group 8 streptococcus","Escherichia coli","Pseudomonas","Candida albicans"],"k":3,"ex":"Case fatality rates in the source: Pseudomonas 74.4%, Candida albicans 43.9%, E. coli 34.0%, group B streptococcus 21.9%, coagulase-negative staphylococci 9.1%.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** CoNS: 9.1%.\n\n- **B.** GBS: 21.9%.\n- **C.** E. coli: 34.0%.\n- **D.** Correct (source key): 74.4%.\n- **E.** Candida: 43.9%.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q75, PDF p.176; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":75,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-076","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other","q":"7 Persistence of the umbilical cord beyond which time should prompt consideration of an underlying abnormality","o":["10 days",".20 days","30 days","40 days","50 days"],"k":2,"ex":"The cord usually separates by about 2 weeks. Per Nelson, persistence beyond 30 days should prompt consideration of an underlying neutrophil functional abnormality (leukocyte adhesion deficiency) or neutropenia.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** 10 days: normal range.\n\n- **B.** 20 days: still within normal variation.\n- **C.** Correct (source key).\n- **D.** 40 days: later than the source threshold.\n- **E.** 50 days: later than the source threshold.\n\nHigh-yield:\nLAD is the classic cause of delayed cord separation.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q76, PDF pp.176-177; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":76,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-080","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"Which common sugar does the clinitest screen not detect?","o":["glucose","fructose","galactose","sucrose","lactose"],"k":3,"ex":"Clinitest detects reducing substances (glucose, fructose, galactose, lactose, pentoses). Sucrose is not a reducing sugar and is not detected.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** Glucose: reducing.\n\n- **B.** Fructose: reducing.\n- **C.** Galactose: reducing.\n- **D.** Correct (source key).\n- **E.** Lactose: reducing.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q80, PDF p.177; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":80,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONATOLOG-083","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Prevention","q":"The antenatal administration of magnesium sulfate before preterm delivery has been suggested to","o":["reduce rates of necrotizing enterocolitis","8. decrease the risk of cerebral palsy in surviving infants","improve overall survival","decrease respiratory distress syndrome in premature infants","reduce rates of intraventricular hemorrhage"],"k":1,"ex":"Antenatal magnesium sulfate before preterm delivery has been suggested to reduce cerebral palsy in survivors, without improving overall survival.","more":"Options:\n- **A.** NEC rates: not reduced.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source key).\n- **C.** Overall survival: not improved.\n- **D.** RDS: not reduced.\n- **E.** IVH: not shown to be reduced.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q83, PDF pp.178-179; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":83,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-005","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"disorders EXCEPT","o":["cataracts","tumor","congenital glaucoma","chorioretinitis retinopathy of prematurity"],"k":2,"ex":"The source explanation states that a cornea larger than 1 cm in diameter in a term infant, together with photophobia and tearing, suggests **congenital glaucoma** (buphthalmos) and needs prompt ophthalmology referral. Raised intraocular pressure stretches the infant eye, so the cornea enlarges and clouds, producing tearing, photophobia and blepharospasm.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. cataracts — lens opacity; absent or abnormal red reflex, can accompany congenital rubella and other congenital infections.\n\n- B. tumor — in infants the relevant tumor is retinoblastoma (leukocoria); the stem does not say which tumor.\n- C. congenital glaucoma — the entity the source explanation describes (enlarged cornea with photophobia and tearing).\n- D. chorioretinitis — typical of congenital TORCH infections (toxoplasmosis, CMV).\n- E. retinopathy of prematurity — a disease of premature infants; label lost in OCR, read by sequence.\n- Whether each option is correct or incorrect cannot be stated, because the stem is missing.\n\nSource explanation:\n5.(C). A cornea >1cm in diameter in a term infant (with photophobia and tearing)\nsuggests congenital glaucoma and requires prompt ophthalmologic consultation.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing; only the fragment 'disorders EXCEPT' survives, so it is unknown whether the key (C) is the exception or the answer. Key is consistent with an 'EXCEPT' item only if the stem concerns causes of leukocoria, which is an inference and not established. Verify the stem in the PDF before deciding.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q5, PDF pp.163-164; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":5,"f":true}]);